{"chunk_id": "knowledge_knowledge_4_watany_guide_part1", "chunk_type": "knowledge", "doc_topic_no": "watany_guide_part1", "text": "You said:\nfinal guide\n\nChatGPT said:\n\nThought for 15s\nHere’s the final guide to back up your Windows local drive D: to a server while ensuring duplicate content is not uploaded twice even if it exists in different folders.\n\nBest solution: Restic (dedupe-by-content + encrypted + fast)\nWhy this matches your requirement: Restic is content-addressed and deduplicates automatically. If the same file content appears in 10 different places on D:, the backup repository stores the data once (it still remembers all paths so restore works normally).\n\n1) Server prep (one-time)\nA) Create a backup user + folder\nOn the server (Linux):\n\nsudo adduser backup\nsudo mkdir -p /srv/restic\nsudo chown backup:backup /srv/restic\nB) Enable SSH key login (recommended)\nOn Windows (PowerShell):\n\nssh-keygen -t ed25519\ntype $env:USERPROFILE\\.ssh\\id_ed25519.pub\nCopy the printed public key into the server:\n\nsudo -u backup mkdir -p /home/backup/.ssh\nsudo -u backup nano /home/backup/.ssh/authorized_keys\nsudo chmod 700 /home/backup/.ssh\nsudo chmod 600 /home/backup/.ssh/authorized_keys\n2) Windows setup (one-time)\nA) Install Restic\nDownload Restic for Windows from the official repo (GitHub “restic” releases).\n\nPut restic.exe somewhere like: C:\\Tools\\restic\\restic.exe\n\nAdd C:\\Tools\\restic\\ to your PATH (optional but helpful).\n\nB) Initialize the repository on the server (first time only)\nIn PowerShell:\n\n$env:RESTIC_REPOSITORY=\"sftp:backup@YOUR_SERVER_IP:/srv/restic\"\n$env:RESTIC_PASSWORD=\"PUT-A-STRONG-PASSWORD-HERE\"\n\nrestic init\n3) Run your first backup of D:\nIn PowerShell:\n\n$env:RESTIC_REPOSITORY=\"sftp:backup@YOUR_SERVER_IP:/srv/restic\"\n$env:RESTIC_PASSWORD=\"PUT-A-STRONG-PASSWORD-HERE\"\n\nrestic backup D:\\ `\n --exclude \"D:\\$RECYCLE.BIN\" `\n --exclude \"D:\\System Volume Information\" `\n --exclude \"D:\\Temp\" `\n --exclude \"**\\node_modules\\**\" `\n --exclude \"**\\.git\\**\"\n✅ Result: duplicates across different folders are deduped automatically in the repo.\n\n4) Verify the backup (strongly recommended)\nrestic snapshots\nrestic check\n5) Set retention rules so the repo doesn’t grow forever\nExample: keep 7 daily, 4 weekly, 12 monthly:\n\nrestic forget --keep-daily 7 --keep-weekly 4 --keep-monthly 12 --prune\n6) Restore test (do this once to confirm you’re safe)\nRestore a small subset to a test folder:\n\nmkdir C:\\RestoreTest | Out-Null\nrestic restore latest --target C:\\RestoreTest --include \"D:\\Photos\"\n7) Automate it (Windows Task Scheduler)\nOption A: simple .ps1 script\nCreate C:\\Tools\\restic\\backup-d.ps1:\n\n$env:RESTIC_REPOSITORY=\"sftp:backup@YOUR_SERVER_IP:/srv/restic\"\n$env:RESTIC_PASSWORD=\"PUT-A-STRONG-PASSWORD-HERE\"\n\n& restic backup D:\\ `\n --exclude \"D:\\$RECYCLE.BIN\" `\n --exclude \"D:\\System Volume Information\" `\n --exclude \"D:\\Temp\" `\n --exclude \"**\\node_modules\\**\" `\n --exclude \"**\\.git\\**\"\n\n& restic forget --keep-daily 7 --keep-weekly 4 --keep-monthly 12 --prune\n& restic check\nThen in Task Scheduler:\n\nTrigger: Daily (e.g., 2:00 AM)\n\nAction: Start a program:\n\nProgram: powershell.exe\n\nArgs: -Execu", "metadata": "{\"source\": \"watany.txt\", \"keywords\": \"watany_guide_part1\"}"}
{"chunk_id": "knowledge_knowledge_5_watany_guide_part2", "chunk_type": "knowledge", "doc_topic_no": "watany_guide_part2", "text": "tionPolicy Bypass -File \"C:\\Tools\\restic\\backup-d.ps1\"\n\nWhat “no duplicate upload regardless of location” really means (and what you’ll get)\nIf D:\\Work\\video.mp4 and D:\\Archive\\video.mp4 are identical:\n\nRestic stores the data once in the server repo.\n\nRestore still recreates both paths exactly.\n\nIf you want an even simpler “set and forget” alternative (GUI)\nDuplicati (easy UI, good for beginners; encryption supported)\n\nKopia (modern, also dedupe + encryption)\n\nBut if your main rule is dedupe across paths, Restic is the cleanest.\n\nIf you tell me what your server is (Linux? Windows? NAS?) and how you connect (SFTP, SMB share, etc.), I’ll adapt the exact commands (paths, permissions, and the best backend) to your setup.\n\nYou said:\n\nguide to read and use as the basis for the development process\n\nChatGPT said:\n\nThought for 20s\nAccording to a document from October 2025, the attached guide is best treated as a procedural handbook made of “transaction cards” (each item = what to do, where to go, what documents are required, conditions, etc.). Your development process should therefore start by turning every procedure into structured fields, then adding correlations (connected procedures) and finally a legal layer (articles + citations) so the chatbot can answer “what you can claim + why + how.”\nchatbot guide\n\nchatbot guide\n\n1) How to read the guide (the “correct mental model”)\nWhen you read each item, extract it as a Transaction Card with these components:\n\nTitle\n\nOwning office / entry point (where to submit)\n\nRequired documents (often with validity windows like “3 months”)\n\nSteps / verifications / special conditions\n\nFees / amounts / deadlines when present\nThis matches exactly how the guide is written and is why it’s perfect for a chatbot KB: it’s already “inputs → checks → outputs.”\nchatbot guide\n\nWhat to normalize while reading\nRepeated documents should become standard document types (don’t rewrite them 65 times). The guide repeats core anchors like civil registry extracts and attestations, so model them once and reference them everywhere.\nchatbot guide\n\nKeep “where to submit” as a structured value (office/entity), not buried text, so the bot can always answer “وين بتقدّم؟” reliably.\nchatbot guide\n\n2) Build the Knowledge Base (KB) as structured data\nMinimum schema you should implement\nsections (the guide’s top-level groupings)\n\ntransactions (one row per procedure/card)\n\ntx_fts (full-text search over title + summary)\n\ntx_links (procedure ↔ procedure correlations)\n\nOptional but recommended: a document_types table and a transaction↔documents join table, so repeated docs stay consistent.\nchatbot guide\n\nchatbot guide\n\nWhat each “transaction” record should contain\nAt minimum (practical for bots + admin review):\n\ntx_no\n\ntitle_ar / title_en (if you add English later)\n\nsummary_ar (structured-ish summary)\n\nwhere_to_submit (office)\n\nrequired_docs[] (references to document types)\n\nsteps[]\n\nfees_lbp (numeric if possible)\n\ndeadlines[]\n\nnotes / exceptions\n\nta", "metadata": "{\"source\": \"watany.txt\", \"keywords\": \"watany_guide_part2\"}"}
{"chunk_id": "knowledge_knowledge_6_watany_guide_part3", "chunk_type": "knowledge", "doc_topic_no": "watany_guide_part3", "text": "gs (agency/topic)\nchatbot guide\n\n3) Add correlations (so the bot suggests the “next related step”)\nThe document identifies that correlations are mostly driven by trigger events (real-life lifecycle changes). Treat these as first-class concepts in your KB and link procedures accordingly:\n\nPrimary correlation triggers:\n\nmarriage / divorce\n\ndeath of a dependent\n\nchild age milestones (18/25) or continuing education\n\nparent becoming eligible/ineligible “على العاتق”\nchatbot guide\n\nHow to implement it\n\nFor each transaction, store:\n\ntriggers[] (from the list above)\n\naffects_entitlements[] (family allowance, pension distribution, etc.)\n\nIn tx_links, create edges like:\n\n“تعديل وضع عائلي” → “تعديل/إيقاف/إعادة تخصيص تعويضات/معاش”\nBecause the guide explicitly describes this cascade pattern.\nchatbot guide\n\n4) Add the legal layer (so answers are defensible)\nThe guide itself points you to the legal engine: it references مرسوم 47/1983 (نظام التقاعد والصرف من الخدمة), and you should also map key procedures to قانون الدفاع الوطني articles (as citations).\nchatbot guide\n\nWhat “mapping” means in your KB\nFor each procedure, attach:\n\nLegal Basis (Primary): which law/article creates the right/constraint\n\nLegal Basis (Operational): which office/committee executes the admin process\nchatbot guide\n\nExample high-value mappings (from the document)\n\n“تعديل وضع عائلي / تعويضات عائلية” → Article 90\n\n“إعادة تخصيص معاش / ورثة / وفاة” → Article 94 (+ 96–97)\n\n“تخصيص معاش تقاعدي أو تعويض صرف” → Article 84\n\n“Workflow ownership / approvals” → Article 99 (process governance)\nchatbot guide\n\nchatbot guide\n\nData model tip\nUse a join table like tx_law_map or legal_citations to connect tx_no ↔ law_code ↔ article_no with a relevance_note (why it’s linked).\nchatbot guide\n\n5) How the chatbot should answer (response rules)\nThe 