sv\n - Outputs: updates transactions, tx_links, rebuilds tx_fts\n\n2) National Defense Law ingestion\n- Implement crawler + parser (MOD site sections) to build law_articles and law_fts:\n - scripts/crawl_national_defense_law.py\n - Must split by “المادة …” and normalize Arabic digits\n - Store into law_sources + law_articles + law_fts\n - Export JSONL for RAG if needed (optional)\n\n3) Procedure ↔ Law mapping generation (first-pass rule-assisted)\n- scripts/generate_tx_to_law_mapping.py\n - Inputs: transactions + law_articles\n - Output: tx_law_map rows with:\n - relevance (0–100)\n - rationale (short Arabic explanation)\n - MUST be regeneratable and then reviewable via admin CSV import/export workflow.\n\nE) Persistence / Storage Decision\n- Primary runtime DB: SQLite KB (kb.sqlite) for now (fast, simple).\n- Optional: If Postgres already exists in current codebase, keep it for app data (users/tickets) BUT KB remains SQLite unless you explicitly add a sync job.\n- If you add Postgres KB, ensure Arabic/English FTS configuration is stable; otherwise default to SQLite FTS to match the KB spec.\n\nF) Quality Bar / Guardrails\n- Arabic-fir", "metadata": "{\"source\": \"watany.txt\", \"keywords\": \"watany_guide_part22\"}"}
{"chunk_id": "knowledge_knowledge_26_watany_guide_part23", "chunk_type": "knowledge", "doc_topic_no": "watany_guide_part23", "text": "st responses (Lebanese formal, not street slang).\n- Never invent legal articles or procedures: if not found, say it’s not in KB and ask for details.\n- Don’t output long verbatim text; provide structured guidance and short previews.\n- Provide consistent answer template:\n\nAnswer Template (Arabic default)\n1) خلاصة سريعة\n2) أين تُقدَّم المعاملة\n3) المستندات المطلوبة\n4) مهلة/ملاحظات زمنية\n5) رسوم/مبالغ (إن وجدت)\n6) خطوات/إجراءات (إن وجدت)\n7) معاملات مرتبطة (التالي المقترح)\n8) الأساس القانوني (مواد قانون الدفاع الوطني ذات الصلة + سبب الربط)\n9) مراجع داخلية: tx_no / article_no\n\nG) “Self-learning / Improvement” Mechanism (controlled)\nImplement feedback capture WITHOUT autonomous editing:\n- POST /api/feedback\n - {message, tx_no?, article_no?, rating (1-5), correction_text?, suggested_mapping?}\nStore feedback in a table/file:\n- /data/feedback.sqlite (or JSONL)\nAdmin can review and then apply changes via import scripts (human-in-the-loop).\n\nH) Documentation\n- README.md:\n - Setup steps\n - How to place KB file\n - Run backend\n - Run frontend\n - Admin login + mapping review\n - How to re-crawl law and regenerate mapping\n- Provide sample .env.example\n\nNow implement all missing files, wire the routes, and ensure the app runs locally:\n- `python -m uvicorn backend.main:app --reload`\n- Frontend served as static files or via a simple file server, or mount `/frontend` via FastAPI StaticFiles.\n\nImportant: Preserve existing code where possible. If there are missing modules, create them. If endpoints exist, extend them to meet the contract above. Ensure all queries are parameterized to avoid SQL injection. Add basic tests for DB connectivity and search endpoints.\nEND.\nIf you want, I can also adapt that same “Copilot master script” to match your exact current repo tree (so Copilot edits the right files instead of creating parallel folders) — but the script above is already aligned with the KB spec in your guide: procedures + correlations + law articles + mapping, with the exact intended search patterns.\nchatbot guide\n\nchatbot guide\n\nchatbot guide\n\nSources\n\nYou said:\ngive me the best and optimum options\n\nChatGPT said:\n\nThought for 11s\nAccording to a document from February 2026, your KB already has a clear “v3” shape: retirees guide procedures (transactions, tx_fts, tx_links) + National Defense Law (law_articles, law_fts) + the procedure→law mapping table (tx_law_map) with relevance + rationale, and the guide explicitly expects SQLite FTS queries like WHERE ... MATCH ....\nchatbot guide\n\nHere are the best / most optimum implementation options, in order of what usually works best in real production for your case.\n\nOption 1 — Lean & Fast (Recommended if you want results immediately)\nKeep the KB as SQLite v3 exactly as defined and build WatanBot as a FastAPI service that queries it directly.\n\nWhat you ship\n\nFastAPI backend + endpoints for:\n\nprocedure search via tx_fts MATCH ...\n\nlaw search via law_fts MATCH ...\n\nprocedure detail that joins tx_law_map to law_articles", "metadata": "{\"source\": \"watany.txt\", \"keywords\": \"watany_guide_part23\"}"}
{"chunk_id": "knowledge_knowledge_27_watany_guide_part24", "chunk_type": "knowledge", "doc_topic_no": "watany_guide_part24", "text": "“related next steps” via tx_links\n\nAdmin mapping review via CSV import/export (because the guide expects human refinement of tx_law_map).\nchatbot guide\n\nWhy it’s optimal\n\nMatches the guide perfectly (no schema translation risk).\n\nFast dev + fast search; minimal moving parts.\n\nYou can iterate the mapping quality quickly (legal review workflow).\n\nTrade-offs\n\nSQLite KB is a file: you must handle backup/versioning and deployment carefully.\n\nBest when\n\nYour top priority is “make it work now” and ship a stable KB-driven assistant.\n\nGrounding from the guide\n\nTables expected in v3 KB and how you query them are explicitly defined.\nchatbot guide\n\nOption 2 — Hybrid Production (Best overall for stability + scaling)\nSQLite remains the canonical KB (as v3), but PostgreSQL stores everything operational: users, sessions, feedback, audit logs, admin edits, and “review queues”.\n\nPattern\n\nSQLite = read-optimized KB/search layer (procedures/law/mapping)\n\nPostgres = write-heavy app layer (feedback/self-learning/human review)\n\nWhy this is the “best overall”\n\nYou keep the guide’s v3 KB intact (so everything stays aligned).\nchatbot guide\n\nYou get proper multi-user concurrency + auditability for the parts that matter operationally (feedback, approvals, admin actions).\n\nYou can later add a “KB sync job” if you decide to migrate KB into Postgres, without rushing it.\n\nTrade-offs\n\nSlightly more setup: Postgres + SQLite in the same app.\n\nRequires clear boundaries (KB is read-only except via controlled import scripts).\n\nBest when\n\nYou want “production behavior” (roles, approvals, audit) without disrupting the KB model.\n\nOption 3 — Full Postgres (Most scalable, most work)\nMigrate the v3 KB schema into Postgres and implement Postgres FTS with the “simple” configuration for bilingual content (as we already enforced in earlier scripts), then re-create the v3 tables and mapping semantics.\n\nPros\n\nOne database, easy backups, easy replication, best for multi-instance deployments.\n\nCons\n\nHighest risk: you must faithfully reproduce the v3 KB behavior and FTS ranking.\n\nMore engineering work up front.\n\nYou’ll need a robust migration + validation layer to ensure results match SQLite FTS behavior.\n\nBest when\n\nYou already know you need to run multiple replicas / heavy traffic / central DB governance.\n\nOption 4 — “Legal Layer Expansion” add-on (regardless of options 1–3)\nThe guide explicitly suggests extending the legal layer beyond National Defense Law by ingesting:\n\nمرسوم 47/1983 (نظام التقاعد والصرف من الخدمة)\n\nقانون العمل\n\nقانون الموظفين\n…and mapping them the same way so each procedure has “what you can claim + why (article) + how to submit”.\nchatbot guide\n\nThis is the most valuable “upgrade path” after you ship v3.\n\nMy recommendation (optimum)\n✅ Go with Option 2 (Hybrid Production):\nKeep the SQLite KB v3 untouched (because the guide defines it exactly).\nchatbot guide\n\nPut auth/roles/audit/feedback/self-learning in Postgres (this is what usually breaks first if you keep it in SQLite", "metadata": "{\"source\": \"watany.txt\", \"keywords\": \"watany_guide_part24\"}"}
