{"chunk_id": "knowledge_knowledge_397_watany_guide_part394", "chunk_type": "knowledge", "doc_topic_no": "watany_guide_part394", "text": "شيء مهم. أنا هنا في أي وقت تحتاج فيه للعودة أو لطرح أي سؤال. لا داعي للعجلة، خذ الأمور بهدوء.\n\nYou said:\n\nmap attched doc and transform pages from 42-64 inot both xls doc and csv\n\nChatGPT said:\n\nThought\n\nYou said:\nnext\n\nChatGPT said:\n\nStopped thinking\n\nYou said:\ncmd to ssh with key\n\nChatGPT said:\nHere are the common SSH commands to connect using a key (and the fixes for the usual errors). Replace values in ALL CAPS.\n\n1) Basic SSH with a private key\nssh -i /path/to/private_key USER@HOST\nExample:\n\nssh -i ~/.ssh/id_rsa root@203.0.113.10\n2) SSH with key + custom port\nssh -i /path/to/private_key -p 2222 USER@HOST\n3) If your key is .pem\nchmod 600 /path/to/key.pem\nssh -i /path/to/key.pem USER@HOST\n4) Use a specific key automatically (per-host config)\nEdit ~/.ssh/config:\n\nHost myserver\n HostName HOST_OR_IP\n User USER\n IdentityFile /path/to/private_key\n IdentitiesOnly yes\n Port 22\nThen connect with:\n\nssh myserver\n5) If you get “UNPROTECTED PRIVATE KEY FILE!”\nFix permissions:\n\nchmod 600 /path/to/private_key\nchmod 700 ~/.ssh\n6) If you must specify the key type (rare)\nssh -o PubkeyAuthentication=yes -o IdentitiesOnly=yes -i /path/to/private_key USER@HOST\n7) Test / debug why key auth fails\nssh -vvv -i /path/to/private_key USER@HOST\n8) If your key is in an agent\neval \"$(ssh-agent -s)\"\nssh-add /path/to/private_key\nssh USER@HOST\nIf you tell me your OS (Windows / Mac / Linux) and whether you have a .pem / id_rsa / ed25519 key, I’ll give you the exact command for your setup.\n\nYou said:\nfrom local computer to server centos\n\nChatGPT said:\nFrom your local computer to a CentOS server, you typically do one of these:\n\n1) SSH into CentOS using a key (most common)\nssh -i /path/to/private_key USER@SERVER_IP\nExamples:\n\nssh -i ~/.ssh/id_ed25519 root@203.0.113.10\nssh -i ~/.ssh/id_rsa centos@203.0.113.10\nIf the server uses a custom port:\n\nssh -i /path/to/private_key -p 2222 USER@SERVER_IP\nIf you get “Permissions too open”\nchmod 600 /path/to/private_key\n2) Copy files from local → CentOS with SCP\nCopy a file:\n\nscp -i /path/to/private_key ./localfile.zip USER@SERVER_IP:/remote/path/\nCopy a folder (recursive):\n\nscp -i /path/to/private_key -r ./localfolder USER@SERVER_IP:/remote/path/\nCustom port:\n\nscp -i /path/to/private_key -P 2222 ./localfile.zip USER@SERVER_IP:/remote/path/\n3) Better file transfer: RSYNC (fast + resumable)\nrsync -avz -e \"ssh -i /path/to/private_key -p 22\" ./localfolder/ USER@SERVER_IP:/remote/path/\n4) If you’re on Windows\nOption A: PowerShell (built-in OpenSSH)\nssh -i C:\\Users\\YOURNAME\\.ssh\\id_ed25519 USER@SERVER_IP\nscp -i C:\\Users\\YOURNAME\\.ssh\\id_ed25519 .\\file.zip USER@SERVER_IP:/remote/path/\nOption B: PuTTY\nConvert your key to .ppk with PuTTYgen, then:\n\nSSH: use PuTTY → Connection → SSH → Auth → browse .ppk\n\nSCP: use pscp.exe:\n\npscp -i C:\\path\\key.ppk .\\file.zip USER@SERVER_IP:/remote/path/\nQuick checklist (so it works first try)\nUsername is often root, centos, or your created user.\n\nKey permissions: chmod 600 key\n\nFirewall/port: confirm port 22 or c", "metadata": "{\"source\": \"watany.txt\", \"keywords\": \"watany_guide_part394\"}"}
